heading('Grades 4-5 / Problem 4')
add('Give a bound on requests and prove the rules finish with everyone paired. Then extend the argument from four per side to ten.')
sub('Exact expected answer: 16 requests; 100 with ten per side')
add('For four askers and four receivers, there are 4 x 4 = 16 distinct asker-receiver requests. No asker repeats a request, so 16 cannot be exceeded. For ten per side, the same reasoning gives 10 x 10 = 100. These are valid upper bounds; neither is asserted to be the smallest possible upper bound.')
table([['Four askers','Receiver W','Receiver X','Receiver Y','Receiver Z'],['A','AW','AX','AY','AZ'],['B','BW','BX','BY','BZ'],['C','CW','CX','CY','CZ'],['D','DW','DX','DY','DZ']],[112,104,104,104,104])
add('One physical proposal marker in a cell records a used request. Every move fills a previously unused cell. This makes the finite bound concrete without assuming children can manipulate n-squared notation.')
sub('The missing step a finite-count argument must include')
add('After a receiver first holds someone, it never becomes empty: a change is an exchange, not a vacancy. Also, an asker is held by at most one receiver, because only free askers make requests. Suppose a free asker had already asked every receiver. Then every receiver would have received a request and would be holding an asker now. Four distinct holds account for all four askers, so no asker can be free. This is a contradiction.')
add('Therefore, whenever some asker is free, it still has an untried receiver. The rules can make another legal request. They cannot do so forever because no request repeats. They must stop with every asker held, and hence with every receiver holding exactly one asker. This proves a perfect pairing, not merely an end to the procedure.')
hint('First count the available request markers. If the child says "therefore it finishes," ask whether an asker could be free with every marker used. What would that tell you about all receivers?')
sub('For any equal size')
add('With n labels on each side there are n x n possible requests. The same nonempty-hold and distinct-asker arguments work for every n. No ordering of the free askers is needed, and no fairness assumption about whose turn comes next is needed in a finite execution: each actual request consumes a new edge.')
