#14
heading('Grades 4-5 / Problem 5')
add('Can two letters in different pairs both prefer one another after the ordinary asking rules finish? <b>No.</b> This page gives the universal proof, not a conclusion drawn from sample runs.')
sub('Two facts to uncover with the physical cards')
table([['Askers move down their own lists','Receivers improve their holds'],['An asker tries choices from first to last and never repeats. Before reaching its eventual partner, it has asked every receiver it ranks higher.','A receiver replaces its hold only with someone it prefers. Once it has rejected an asker, every later hold is preferred to that rejected asker.']],[264,264])
sub('Proof by considering an alleged blocking pair')
add('Call the asker p and the receiver r. Suppose p prefers r to its final partner. Because requests follow p\'s ranking, p must already have asked r before asking its final partner. Since they are not together at the end, r either rejected p immediately or held p and released it later.')
add('At that rejection, r kept another asker q whom it preferred to p. Afterward, r\'s hold could only improve. Its final partner is therefore preferred to p as well. So r does <b>not</b> prefer p to its final partner. The two preferences required for a blocker cannot both hold. This argument applies to every unmatched p-r pair, proving stability.')
table([['Possible history at r','What r\'s preference tells us'],['p asks; r already holds q and rejects p.','q is ahead of p in r\'s strip.'],['Or: r holds p; q arrives and p is released.','Again q is ahead of p.'],['Later r keeps q or exchanges it for better holds.','The final hold is still ahead of p.']],[264,264])
hint('Choose an asker who claims to want a better final partner. "Did it ever ask that receiver? What happened there? Could that receiver\'s hold get worse afterward?" Let the child supply the link between the two lists.')
sub('What counts as a complete explanation')
add('Accept a spoken argument with cards, a chronological drawing, or a written proof. It must connect <b>the asker\'s earlier request</b> to <b>the receiver\'s final preference</b>. "We tried lots of lists" and "receivers get better" alone are incomplete. Combining this result with Problem 4 proves that a stable perfect matching exists for every strict, complete, equal-sized two-sided instance.')
add('<b>Boundary of the guarantee.</b> Do not carry this proof unchanged into ties, omitted choices, unequal sides, multiple-place capacities, or one-set roommate matching. Those are different models. The cards represent artificial objects; the exercise makes no claim about measuring real people\'s happiness or fairness.')
