heading('Grades 4-5 / Problem 6')
add('Make a stable three-pair matching with as many last-choice recipients as possible. <b>The maximum is three of the six letters.</b> This does not require proposer optimality.')
profiles('last3')
diagram(['AX/BY/CZ'],left='ABC',right='XYZ',titles=['AX / BY / CZ: circles first, squares last'])
add('<b>Construction:</b> choose AX / BY / CZ. Every circle receives its first choice, so no circle prefers any alternative and there can be no blocker. X receives A, its last choice; Y receives B, its last; Z receives C, its last. Thus three last-choice recipients are possible.')
sub('Why four cannot occur')
add('If four of the six letters received last choices, last-choice recipients would occur on both sides because each side has only three letters. Among those recipients, choose a circle and a square who are <b>not paired to one another</b>. Such a pair must exist: if there is just one last-choice circle, there are three last-choice squares and it is paired with at most one of them; if there are two or more on each side, any chosen circle has at least one nonpartner among those squares. The case with one square is symmetric.')
add('Each of the selected nonpartners prefers <b>every</b> other opposite-side label to its current last-choice partner. In particular, each prefers the other. They form a blocking pair, contradicting stability. The same reasoning rules out five or six: any set of at least four contains such opposite-side nonpartners. Therefore the exact maximum is three.')
hint('Try giving a whole side its first choices. For the upper bound, place a marker on each last-choice recipient and ask whether a marked circle and a marked square in different pairs could coexist.')
sub('Independent numerical check, not the proof')
add('All 46,656 complete strict three-pair preference profiles and their six candidate matchings were checked independently: 279,936 candidates in total, 60,324 stable outcomes counted over the profiles, and a largest last-choice count of 3. The contradiction above explains why; the finite audit only cross-checks it.')
