#16
heading('Optional depth and responsive facilitation')
sub('Extension A / Does the free-asker order matter?')
add('It does not change the final pairing for a fixed proposing side in this model. This is optional, quantifier-heavy work. Use it only when children can distinguish a pair in <i>some</i> stable matching from a pair in the current tentative process.')
add('<b>First-rejection proof.</b> Assume there is a first rejection of a pair that belongs to at least one stable matching. Call the rejected asker p, the receiver r, and the kept asker q; choose a stable matching S containing p-r. Since r chose q, r prefers q to p. If q preferred its S-partner to r, q would have asked that partner earlier and been rejected before reaching r. That would be an earlier rejection of a pair belonging to a stable matching, contrary to the choice of the first rejection. Also q\'s S-partner cannot be r, since S pairs r with p. Hence q prefers r to its S-partner. Then q-r blocks S, a contradiction.')
add('Thus no pair occurring in any stable matching is ever rejected. Every receiver ahead of an asker\'s final partner has rejected it. None could be its partner in a stable matching. Therefore every asker obtains its most-preferred partner among <b>all stable matchings</b>. Strict preferences make each such best partner unique, so changing the order of free askers cannot change the outcome. Reversing the proposing side can change it, as Problem 2 shows.')
hint('Return to the two-stable two-pair example first. Ask what "best possible while still stable" means before attempting the general proof. Do not relabel this as best for both sides or maximum total satisfaction.')
sub('Extension B / More last choices')
add('For n >= 3, the maximum possible number of last-choice recipients is n. Give all askers distinct first choices and place each chosen asker last on its receiver\'s list; that supplies n. If more than n are last, both sides contain last-choice recipients and an opposite-side nonpartner pair exists, which blocks. For n = 2 the maximum is also 2 (one first-choice side construction); for n = 1 both labels simultaneously have their first and last choice, so the answer is 2. Keep this boundary case if generalizing.')
sub('Observe before revising')
add('Record the actual date, participants and packet version; problems offered and attempted; whether children checked both preferences without rescue; which representation helped; any cumulative-strip mistake; whether request markers prevented repeated requests; and what children wanted to continue. Separate observations from guesses about why they occurred. Record this slot as used only after an actual meeting, and keep its draft/unpiloted status until then.')
add('<b>Misconceptions worth pausing for:</b> "everybody gets a first choice"; a unilateral wish counted as a blocker; crossing lines judged illegal; preferences changed halfway through a run; or temporary holds treated as permanent. Fix the concrete rule with one example, then return ownership of the investigation to the children.','SmallX')
